{"object_id":"org.5d1a575385a05456","object_type":"network","slug":"cbs-pacific-network","title":"CBS Pacific Network","paragraphs":["The CBS Pacific Network label in this archive points to CBS broadcasts and production activity tied to the West Coast, where Hollywood became an increasingly important source of network radio.","In Golden Radio Hour, [[org|org.5d1a575385a05456|CBS Pacific Network]] is tied to 5 programs. The surviving network-linked recordings here run from 1933 to 1955.","Programs with a particularly strong presence here include [[show|the-whistler|The Whistler]], [[show|calling-all-cars|Calling All Cars]], [[show|rocky-jordan|Rocky Jordan]], [[show|all-star-western-theatre|All Star Western Theatre]], and [[show|jeff-regan-investigator|Jeff Regan, Investigator]].","People who recur often in those network-linked recordings include Wilbur Hatch, Marvin Miller, George W. Allen, Charles Frederick Lindsley, Jesse Rosenquist, Bill Forman, Richard Aurandt, [[person|jack-moyles|Jack Moyles]], Cliff Howell, and Foy Willing and The Riders Of The Purple Sage. These are archive connections, not a claim that everyone on the list was employed directly by the network."],"plain_text":"The CBS Pacific Network label in this archive points to CBS broadcasts and production activity tied to the West Coast, where Hollywood became an increasingly important source of network radio.\n\nIn Golden Radio Hour, CBS Pacific Network is tied to 5 programs. The surviving network-linked recordings here run from 1933 to 1955.\n\nPrograms with a particularly strong presence here include The Whistler, Calling All Cars, Rocky Jordan, All Star Western Theatre, and Jeff Regan, Investigator.\n\nPeople who recur often in those network-linked recordings include Wilbur Hatch, Marvin Miller, George W. Allen, Charles Frederick Lindsley, Jesse Rosenquist, Bill Forman, Richard Aurandt, Jack Moyles, Cliff Howell, and Foy Willing and The Riders Of The Purple Sage. These are archive connections, not 